payload.message:
For your setup, SSH agent is the missing piece that keeps you from typing the key path or passphrase repeatedly.

Right now I added:

```nix
addKeysToAgent = "yes";
```

That tells SSH: “when I use this key, offer to load it into the agent.” But it assumes an agent is actually running.

In Home Manager, the usual declarative setup is to enable the SSH agent itself:

```nix
programs.ssh = {
  enable = true;
  startAgent = true;
  matchBlocks = {
    "github.com" = {
      user = "git";
      hostname = "github.com";
      identityFile = "~/.ssh/ansible_razer";
      identitiesOnly = true;
      addKeysToAgent = "yes";
    };
  };
};
```

What this does in plain English:

- `startAgent = true` starts an `ssh-agent` for your login session
- `identityFile` says which key to use for GitHub
- `addKeysToAgent = "yes"` lets SSH load that key into the agent
- after that, Git operations can reuse the key without you manually running `ssh-add` every time

Two practical notes:

- If `ansible_razer` has no passphrase, the agent mostly just helps manage the key automatically.
- If it does have a passphrase, the agent is much more useful because you usually enter it once per session instead of repeatedly.

If you want tighter control, you can also add:

```nix
extraConfig = ''
  Host *
    AddKeysToAgent yes
'';
```

But for your case, keeping it only on `github.com` is cleaner.

If you want, I can patch [home.nix](/home/pablo/dotfiles/home.nix:1) now to add `startAgent = true`.

